The three screenshots provided — Hamzah-Sprint-2-Screenshot-1, Hamzah-Sprint-2-Screenshot-2, and Hamzah-Sprint-2-Screenshot-3 — illustrate the progression of our Scrum board throughout the sprint.

In Hamzah-Sprint-2-Screenshot-1, the board is mostly empty, with user stories remaining in the “To Do” column and no tasks actively in progress. This represents the starting point of the sprint before any work has begun.

In Hamzah-Sprint-2-Screenshot-2, all group members’ user stories have been added to the board. At this stage, I moved my own user story from the “To Do” column into the “In Progress” column, indicating that I had started working on it.

In Hamzah-Sprint-2-Screenshot-3, my user story has been completed and moved into the “Done” column. After finishing my task, I moved the next person’s user story into the “In Progress” column, demonstrating the continuation of workflow within the team.

Overall, these screenshots show the movement of tasks across the Scrum board, from initial planning, to active development, and finally to completion.